  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current best solution achieves a bound of 0.38085, and the goal is to improve it to 0.380876 or lower. The existing approach uses local optimization with random restarts and grid refinement, which may get stuck in local optima. This strategy employs a multi-resolution optimization combined with a hybrid optimization method to escape local optima. We start with the parent solution (n=47) and refine it to finer grids (n=94, 188, 376) using interpolation and repair mechanisms. The key improvements are:\n1. Using a global optimizer (CMA-ES) for exploration and a local optimizer (SLSQP) for refinement.\n2. Implementing a repair function that ensures feasibility while preserving the integral constraint.\n3. Allocating decreasing budgets per grid size to stay within the 1100-second limit.\n4. Transferring solutions between grids via interpolation and repair.\n\nThe approach addresses the bottleneck of local optimization by leveraging grid refinement and hybrid methods. The repair mechanism ensures both constraints are satisfied, and the hybrid optimization balances exploration and exploitation. The time budget is carefully allocated to maximize the number of evaluations while minimizing runtime.\n\nMathematical specification\nThe problem is to minimize the maximum overlap integral \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))  dx \\) subject to \\( h(x) \\in [0,1] \\) and \\( \\int_0^2 h(x)  dx = 1 \\).\n\nDiscrete representation:\n- Represent \\( h \\) as \\( n \\) point samples on [0,2] with \\( \\Delta x = 2/n \\).\n- Constraints: \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) for all \\( i \\) and \\( \\sum h[i] = n/2 \\).\n- Objective: Compute \\( \\max_k \\) of the correlation between \\( h \\) and \\( 1-h \\), scaled by \\( \\Delta x \\).\n\nOptimization formulation:\n- Variables: \\( h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\) with box constraints [0,1]^n and equality constraint \\( \\sum h[i] = n/2 \\).\n- Objective: Non-convex, defined via correlation.\n- Repair: Clip to [0,1] and adjust sum uniformly.\n\nGrid transfer:\n- Interpolate parent solution to finer grids.\n- Repair sum and bounds post-interpolation.\n\nSolver selection:\n- Use CMA-ES for global exploration on coarse grids.\n- Use SLSQP for local refinement on finer grids.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Set random seed and retrieve initial_h_values if provided.\n   - Initialize best_h, best_c5, and time tracking.\n   - Define grid sizes and time budgets.\n\n2. Grid loop:\n   - For each grid size n, allocate time budget.\n   - Generate multiple restarts (5 per grid) if parent solution available, otherwise random.\n   - Repair initial guesses to satisfy constraints.\n\n3. Optimization:\n   - For each restart, choose solver (CMA-ES for n<=100, SLSQP otherwise).\n   - Define objective and constraint functions.\n   - Set bounds and options for solver.\n   - Run optimizer and track time.\n\n4. Repair and evaluation:\n   - Repair optimized solution.\n   - Compute C5 and update best solution if improved.\n   - Handle grid transfer by interpolation and repair.\n\n5. Termination:\n   - Break if time budget exceeded.\n   - Return best_h, best_c5, and n_points.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n- Grid sizes: [47, 94, 188, 376].\n- Restart strategy: 5 restarts per grid, parent as first guess if available.\n- Solver selection: CMA-ES for n<=100, SLSQP otherwise.\n- Time budget: [500, 300, 250, 50] seconds per grid.\n- Repair: Clip to [0,1] and adjust sum uniformly.\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n- Dominant cost: Correlation computation (O(n^2)) and optimization.\n- Estimates: n=47: ~100s per restart, n=94: ~60s, n=188: ~50s, n=376: ~10s.\n- Budget allocation: 500s, 300s, 250s, 50s per grid.\n- Robustness: Repair ensures feasibility, fallback to parent if no improvement.\n\nValidation and return contract\n- Feasibility check: Ensure 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)=n/2.\n- Objective consistency: Re-evaluate best candidate.\n- Output: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where h_values is the best solution array.\n\nThe implementation uses scipy.optimize.minimize with CMA-ES and SLSQP, numpy for correlation and repair, and careful time tracking. The repair function ensures joint feasibility, and grid transfer maintains solution quality across resolutions.",
